# Full-depth felt-experience: dissociatives and anesthetics.
from gen import D
F={}
F["nitrous-oxide"]={
"onset":["Nitrous oxide ('laughing gas', 'whippets', 'nos') is a gas that acts within seconds of being breathed in. The first sensation is a rising, buzzing tingle in the lips, fingers and toes, a warm heaviness in the limbs and a rhythmic, pulsing 'wah-wah' sound as hearing distorts and echoes.","Mentally the onset is giddy and dreamlike: laughter bubbles up easily, the world feels distant and funny, and thoughts begin to drift and loop. Some feel a sudden sense of profound insight that evaporates moments later, a hallmark of the gas.","Breathing it straight from a pressurized canister or tank can freeze the lips and throat and damage the lungs, and breathing it with no fresh air can starve the brain of oxygen. Standing up while inhaling leads to falls and head injuries when the legs give way."],
"peak":["At its peak, which lasts only a minute or so, the body can feel weightless or very heavy, sound becomes a deep pulsing drone, vision may blur or tunnel, and coordination is lost. Some people go briefly numb and slump.","Mentally the peak is a short dissociative dream: the sense of self loosens, the room seems far away, and some people experience vivid fleeting visions or a feeling of touching a great truth. Laughter, confusion and a sense of being unable to finish a thought are common.","Fainting, falls, vomiting while dazed and oxygen deprivation are the main immediate dangers. Using it in cars, near water or with balloons over the face has led to deaths."],
"plateau":["Nitrous oxide has almost no plateau unless it is inhaled repeatedly. With repeated breaths, the body stays tingly, numb and unsteady, the lips may feel cold, and dizziness and headache build. Repeated use keeps oxygen lower and raises the risk of fainting.","Mentally repeated breaths keep a person in a looping, giggly, disconnected state. Some feel the novelty fade and chase the first rush, taking more in quick succession, which mostly adds confusion rather than pleasure.","Heavy repeated use inactivates vitamin B12, and over weeks this can damage the spinal cord and nerves, causing numbness, tingling, weak legs and difficulty walking. Hospitals in several countries have reported rising numbers of young people with this nerve damage."],
"comedown":["The effect clears within a few minutes, leaving a light-headed, slightly dazed feeling, sometimes a headache or nausea. After a long session, people may feel tired, cold and wobbly.","Mentally there is a quick return to normal, sometimes with a vague sense of having forgotten something important or embarrassment about laughing. Insights during the peak rarely survive.","Anyone who notices tingling, numbness, balance problems or weakness after regular use should stop and see a doctor, because B12-related nerve damage can be treated if caught early. Mixing nitrous with alcohol or other depressants adds risk of passing out and vomiting."]}
F["propofol"]={
"onset":["Propofol is a milky-white intravenous anesthetic used to put people to sleep for surgery and procedures. Given into a vein, it acts within about the length of a few breaths: there can be a brief sting in the arm, then a warm, heavy, pleasant drowsiness rushing over the body as the room seems to recede.","Mentally the onset is a smooth slide toward unconsciousness. Some people feel a brief, intense euphoria or a sense of calm release, and many report pleasant dreams or a feeling of having had a deep, restful sleep. That pleasantness is part of why some healthcare workers have misused it.","Propofol is used only in monitored settings because breathing can slow or stop within moments and blood pressure can fall. Outside a hospital there is no safe way to use it; the death of Michael Jackson in 2009 is a widely known example of propofol given without proper monitoring."],
"peak":["At its peak a person is unconscious and unresponsive, with relaxed muscles, slow shallow breathing and lowered blood pressure. Anesthetists support breathing and watch oxygen and heart rhythm continuously.","There is no conscious experience at the peak for most people. A few remember dreams, often pleasant, sometimes sexual or vivid. Lighter sedation can leave people dreamy, disinhibited and talkative without later memory.","The main dangers are stopped breathing, airway blockage, low blood pressure and heart rhythm problems. Propofol infusion syndrome, with muscle breakdown and heart failure, can occur with long, high infusions in intensive care."],
"plateau":["During a procedure, propofol is given continuously to keep a person asleep. The body stays limp and still, breathing is supported or watched closely, and the person feels nothing of the surgery.","Mentally there is nothing remembered, or perhaps a sense of a pleasant, timeless gap. Those misusing it in short bursts describe chasing the quick dreamy release again and again.","In misuse, the fine line between sedation and stopped breathing is easily crossed, and many healthcare workers who misused it have been found dead with the infusion still running. Treatment programs exist for clinicians with this problem."],
"comedown":["Waking from propofol is usually quick and clear, within minutes of stopping. People often feel refreshed, sometimes cold or shivery, with a dry mouth, and occasionally sore at the site where it was given.","Mentally many people wake feeling calm, sometimes giggly or briefly euphoric, with little hangover compared with older anesthetics. Some feel briefly confused or emotional, especially older adults.","Because waking is pleasant and quick, compulsive misuse can develop fast among people with access. Any misuse is extremely dangerous and needs confidential professional help."]}
F["etomidate"]={
"onset":["Etomidate is an intravenous anesthetic used in emergency rooms to put people to sleep quickly without lowering blood pressure much. In recent years, etomidate added to vape liquids ('space oil', 'zombie' vapes) has spread in parts of East and Southeast Asia. Given into a vein, it acts within seconds, often with a brief burning in the arm and twitching of the muscles.","Mentally the onset is a sudden drop toward sleep. Vaped, people describe a fast, heavy, dreamy dissociation, dizziness, and a feeling of the body becoming far away, followed by confusion.","Muscle jerks (myoclonus) are a known effect and can look like a seizure. In vapes the amount and purity are unknown, and reports describe people collapsing, losing control of movement or behaving like 'zombies.'"],
"peak":["At its peak in medical use, the person is unconscious with relaxed muscles and fairly stable blood pressure. In vape misuse, people may sway, stagger, twitch and fall, with slurred speech and blank, slow responses.","Mentally there is little memory of the peak. Those misusing it report a detached, numbing trance with confusion and loss of time.","Etomidate suppresses the adrenal glands' ability to make cortisol, the body's stress hormone. With repeated use, this can lead to low blood pressure, low sodium, weakness and even life-threatening adrenal crisis."],
"plateau":["Medically the effect is brief, and it is usually given once. Repeated vaping keeps people dizzy, unsteady, twitchy and drowsy, with nausea and vomiting and a risk of falling.","Mentally repeated use keeps a person in a foggy, disconnected state, with poor memory and slowed thinking, and craving can drive frequent use throughout the day.","Long-term misuse has been linked to adrenal failure, involuntary movements, psychiatric symptoms and dependence. Medical teams need to know about etomidate use because it changes how they treat low blood pressure."],
"comedown":["Waking from medical etomidate is fairly quick, sometimes with nausea, vomiting and muscle aches. After vape misuse people may feel weak, faint, nauseated and shaky.","Mentally there can be confusion, low mood, anxiety and strong cravings, especially after frequent use.","People who stop after long misuse may need doctors to check and support adrenal function, because sudden adrenal failure can be dangerous. Counseling and addiction services help with the craving."]}
F["esketamine"]={
"onset":["Esketamine (Spravato) is a nasal spray made of one mirror-image half of ketamine, approved for treatment-resistant depression and depression with suicidal thoughts. It is given only in a certified clinic, under observation. Within minutes, many people feel a light, floating tingle, numbness in the mouth and a mild heaviness in the arms and legs.","Mentally the onset can feel dreamy and detached: sounds may seem distant or echoing, time stretches, and some people feel slightly unreal or separated from their body. For others it is barely noticeable beyond drowsiness.","Blood pressure often rises for an hour or two, which is checked in the clinic. Nausea, dizziness and an odd taste are common."],
"peak":["At its peak, roughly within the first hour, the body may feel heavy or floaty, coordination is impaired, and some people feel numb, sleepy or nauseated. Patients are kept seated or reclining and are not allowed to drive afterward.","Mentally there can be marked dissociation: feeling outside one's body, vivid daydreams, distorted sense of time or space, and sometimes anxiety. Many people find it strange but tolerable; some find it pleasant or emotionally meaningful.","Sedation, raised blood pressure and dissociation are why it is restricted. Misuse potential exists, which is why take-home use is not allowed."],
"plateau":["The effects wind down over an hour or two in the clinic. Dizziness, drowsiness and mild nausea may linger, and the body slowly regains steadiness.","Mentally the dissociation fades into a quiet, tired calm. Some people notice mood relief within hours to days, part of what makes esketamine valuable in severe depression.","Staff check blood pressure and alertness before release. A ride home is required."],
"comedown":["Afterward many people feel tired and may want to sleep, with occasional headache.","Mentally there may be a lifting of depression over the following days, though benefits usually need repeated sessions and continuing other treatment.","Bladder problems and dependence, seen in heavy recreational ketamine use, are a concern but appear uncommon with supervised medical dosing."]}
F["methoxetamine"]={
"onset":["Methoxetamine (MXE) is a designer dissociative related to ketamine, sold as a research chemical from around 2010 until bans in many countries. It comes on more slowly than ketamine, often taking a while to build, which leads some people to redose before it has kicked in. Early on there is a light, tingling body buzz, unsteady movement and a warm, loose feeling.","Mentally the onset is a gradual drift into detachment, with a mood lift that many found more euphoric and longer-lasting than ketamine. Music seems richer and thoughts feel interesting.","Delayed onset and strong potency made accidental heavy dosing common, and emergency departments saw people with agitation, confusion and fast heart rates."],
"peak":["At its peak the body is heavy or numb, coordination is poor and walking difficult. Heart rate and blood pressure often rise, unlike pure sedatives.","Mentally there can be deep dissociation: the self and the room fragment, time distorts, and some enter a 'hole' state of profound disconnection with dreamlike visions. Some people feel euphoric, others frightened or paranoid.","Risks include falls, choking on vomit, high blood pressure, agitation and accidents. Mixing with alcohol or depressants raises the danger of breathing problems."],
"plateau":["The plateau is long, several hours, with the body heavy and clumsy, the eyes sometimes flicking side to side, and nausea possible.","Mentally many feel calm, thoughtful and detached, sometimes with mood lifting that lingers. Others feel stuck in confusing, looping thoughts.","Bladder damage, similar to ketamine's, and long-term memory problems are concerns with frequent use."],
"comedown":["The comedown can be slow, with tiredness, headache and lingering unsteadiness.","Mentally some feel low, flat or anxious, while others feel brighter for a day. Frequent use has been linked to depression, dependence and psychosis.","Avoid driving until fully clear, and seek help for bladder pain or blood in urine."]}
F["rc-dissociatives"]={
"onset":["'Research chemical' dissociatives include compounds like 3-MeO-PCP, 3-HO-PCP, O-PCE, deschloroketamine (DCK), 2F-DCK and diphenidine. Their onsets vary widely, from fast to slow; typically there is a light, floating tingle, numbness and loss of coordination as the drug builds.","Mentally most create detachment, with a sense of watching oneself from a distance and changed perception of time and space. Some, like 3-MeO-PCP, are notably stimulating and mood-lifting; others are more sedating.","Because many are much stronger than ketamine and poorly studied, tiny differences in amount can cause unexpectedly intense effects. Slow onsets lead to dangerous redosing."],
"peak":["At their peak the body may be numb, heavy and hard to move, with a raised heart rate and blood pressure. Some people become agitated, others nearly unconscious.","Mentally there may be deep dissociation, confusion, vivid inner imagery and loss of sense of self. With PCP-like compounds, mania, aggression and psychosis are possible.","Overheating, seizures, high blood pressure and dangerous behavior have been reported. Mixing with alcohol or depressants adds risk of choking and stopped breathing."],
"plateau":["Plateaus can last many hours with some compounds. The body stays clumsy and unsteady, and nausea may come and go.","Mentally people can be stuck in a confused, detached state for a long time, which can be frightening. Some develop sleeplessness and manic energy that builds over days of use.","Psychosis and mania after repeated use have been reported, especially with 3-MeO-PCP."],
"comedown":["The comedown can be slow and uneven, with tiredness, headache and lingering unsteadiness.","Mentally low mood, anxiety and confusion are common, and some people feel strange or unreal for days.","Bladder damage and dependence have been linked to frequent use of ketamine-like analogues."]}
for k,v in F.items():
    if k in D: D[k].setdefault("feel",{}).update(v)
